2. Open JTalk runtime dictionary: three-clause BSD, downloaded and not redistributed here
====================================================================================

`KBJapaneseKit` downloads an Open JTalk dictionary on first use. It is not committed in this
repository, and it is not Apache-2.0.

  Archive:  open_jtalk_dic_utf_8-1.11.tar.gz
  Release:  Open JTalk v1.11.1
            https://github.com/r9y9/open_jtalk/releases/tag/v1.11.1
  SHA-256:  fe6ba0e43542cef98339abdffd903e062008ea170b04e7e2a35da805902f382a

Three three-clause BSD notices apply, one per part of the work:

  - The Nara Institute of Science and Technology (NAIST), for the NAIST Japanese Dictionary.
  - The UniDic Consortium, for the UniDic material carried in it.
  - The Nagoya Institute of Technology, Department of Computer Science, and the HTS Working
    Group, for Open JTalk itself. That notice is reproduced verbatim in
    `Licenses/OpenJTalk-Engine-BSD.txt`.

The archive carries its own complete `COPYING`, and that file is the authoritative text. The
extractor writes every regular archive entry to disk, so `COPYING` is installed with the
dictionary data and is never filtered out. Full detail, including what the extractor does and
does not skip, is in `Licenses/OpenJTalk-Dictionary-NOTICES.md`.

What a consuming app must do. Once the dictionary is on a user's device you have
redistributed it. Reproduce all three notices, in full, from the archive's `COPYING` in your
documentation or About material; keep the non-endorsement clauses intact; and leave `COPYING`
in place in the installed directory. The upstream release directs users to that file rather
than restating it, and so does this notice: a paraphrase is not a licence.


